==== <span class="anchor" id="Review"></span> Canned request for source assessment ====
{{shortcut|WP:AIREVIEW}}

When confronted by a declined draft, or otherwise criticism of their work, it is common for LLM editors to make a stock request for an "experienced," "uninvolved," or "neutral" editor to provide a second opinion of their sources or their writing in general. This usually carries the (unintentional?) implication that the declining reviewer [[WP:EGGS|didn't do their job properly]].

{{blockquote|Could {{highlight|an experienced editor}} please advise which of these sources, if any, {{highlight|count as reliable, independent, significant coverage for a biography article?}}
|From {{section link|Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2026 May 8#15:09, 8 May 2026 review of submission by GMakwana}}
}}

{{blockquote|Could {{highlight|an uninvolved editor}} advise which of the current references, if any, {{highlight|count as significant independent secondary coverage}} for notability purposes, and which should be treated only as supporting or weak sources?
|From {{section link|Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2026 May 15#21:10, 15 May 2026 review of submission by Mike1582}}
}}

Notice how these sound like almost exactly the same comment? These were written by two completely different people.

{{blockquote|I would like clarification about the reliability and sufficiency of the sources I added. {{highlight|Could you please review my listed sources and let me know which ones qualify as reliable, independent coverage}} and which ones do not meet Wikipedia’s standards?
|From {{section link|Wikipedia:WikiProject Articles for creation/Help desk/Archives/2025 December 2#08:02, 2 December 2025 review of submission by GTays}}
}}

'''Links to searches'''
* {{plain link|url=https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=%22advise+which+of%22+%22if+any%2C+count+as%22+%22coverage+for%22&title=Special%3ASearch&profile=advanced&fulltext=1&ns4=1 "advise which of" "if any, count as" "coverage for"}}

=== <span class="anchor" id="Complaints about speculation"></span> Complaints about accusers acting on speculation ===
{{shortcuts|WP:CAAAOS|WP:SPECULATIONABOUTAIUSE}}
{{tmbox|image=none|text=Words to watch: {{strong|''speculation/speculative/speculating'', ''stylistic indications'', ''unsubstantiated/unsupported/unwarranted''}} }}
When a user is confronted for allegedly pasting content from a large language model, they will likely dismiss these concerns as unsubstantiated speculation based on tone or [[WP:STYLEDOESN'TMEANAI|writing style]] rather than [[WP:NOPROOFOFAI|hard evidence]] of LLM use. Most if not all users who do so also tell those who confront them to just [[WP:ORCC|point out what needs to be improved]] and [[WP:LETSFOCUSON|focus on that instead of calling them out]].

'''Examples'''
{{blockquote|{{fmbox|image=none|text=
Your message reeks of arrogance, ignorance, and an unhealthy reliance on flawed AI tools. First, GPTZero is notoriously unreliable in distinguishing AI-generated from human-written text, so throwing that into the mix as your "evidence" is laughable at best. If you can't back up your claims with solid proof, you're just flinging {{highlight|baseless accusations}}.

You claim that I’ve breached policies repeatedly, yet {{highlight|fail to specify a single instance or cite evidence}}. It's easy to make {{highlight|vague allegations}}, but hard to stand up to scrutiny when you're put on the spot, isn't it? Wikipedia's policies aren't designed to be weaponized by people like you who clearly have an agenda.

As for your claim about my alleged conflict of interest and accusations of paid editing, it's incredible how quickly people like you jump to conclusions based on {{highlight|incomplete, off-wiki assumptions}}. You’re so certain about these accusations, yet {{highlight|conveniently leave out details}}. Perhaps you're confusing {{highlight|circumstantial gossip}} with facts? Until you have hard, undeniable proof, you might want to rethink your accusations.

Also, if you think you can disregard someone’s unblock request based on an {{highlight|unproven assumption about its origin}}, you clearly don't understand Wikipedia’s principles of fairness and due process. Do better.
----
It's ironic how you claim to be fighting against promotional abuse while making {{highlight|broad assumptions}} about me based on information from off-wiki sources. Wikipedia is built on verifiability and transparency, but your accusations are grounded in {{highlight|speculation and circumstantial evidence}}. You still haven't provided clear proof or pointed to specific instances of policy violations, instead relying on {{highlight|vague claims}} about information I supposedly "placed elsewhere on the internet." Where is the concrete evidence linking me to these subjects?

You say you have no agenda, yet your eagerness to cast me in a negative light {{highlight|without solid proof}} suggests otherwise. If you're so confident in your claims, why not present them with clarity and precision? Wikipedia's mission is to be fair, and any actions against an editor should be based on facts, not {{highlight|assumptions or innuendo}}.

[...] If you believe there's a legitimate concern, then bring it forward in a transparent and verifiable way. Otherwise, these continued {{highlight|unfounded accusations}} serve only to derail productive discussion. And now that they blocked me I can't even contribute anymore anyway why putting in effort if then is removed and not appreciated.
----
It's unfortunate how some are quick to accuse others {{highlight|without offering any actual proof}}. I want to make it clear that I have never been paid for any edits, and it’s disappointing that such assumptions are being made {{highlight|without concrete evidence}}. It's easy to say "evidence exists" {{highlight|without presenting any}}, but making such accusations {{highlight|without proof}} is unfair and harmful.

I understand you're stating that another user blocked me, but {{highlight|that doesn’t make the accusations any more valid}}. What’s truly sad is that this whole situation {{highlight|seems to stem from speculation rather than fact}}. It's important to recognize that while discussions may have taken place, {{highlight|assumptions and accusations}} don't replace genuine evidence.

Being blocked for {{highlight|an unfounded reason}} is frustrating, but this whole narrative that I've violated policies or that there’s some kind of “paid conflict of interest” is simply untrue. I take editing and community guidelines seriously, and to see my actions misrepresented like this is disappointing.
}}|From [[User talk:Damjana12#October 2024|multiple October 2024 comments]] at [[User talk:Damjana12]]}}

{{blockquote|{{fmbox|image=none|text=
To clarify, all portions of the article have undergone comprehensive editorial scrutiny, including thorough review, proper citation of reliable sources, and meticulous fact-checking using a wide range of high-quality, verifiable publications. No evidence of AI-generated content is in the current version, and {{highlight|no findings of AI assistance have been substantiated}}.

[...]

The phrasing “may have” was used in good faith as a transparent acknowledgment of potential early drafting methods. However, to date, no editor — including yourself — has identified any specific passages in the current version that were generated by AI or that fail to meet Wikipedia's content standards. As such, the suggestion that the article contains problematic AI-derived material {{highlight|remains unsubstantiated}}.

[...]

Given the article’s current state — well-sourced, policy-compliant, and collaboratively improved — the continued presence of the “LLM advisory” banner {{highlight|is unwarranted}}. Naturally, editorial vigilance should remain ongoing, as with any living article.
----
As per WP:BURDEN, the responsibility to substantiate claims — including allegations of AI-generated or otherwise non-compliant content — lies squarely with the editor raising the concern. To date, {{highlight|no diff, specific passage, or reliable source has been presented to support such claims}}.

[...]

Without specific, actionable concerns tied to the present revision, maintaining a warning banner is not aligned with WP:NOTCRYSTAL ({{highlight|speculative concerns}}) or WP:UNDUE, and risks misleading readers by implying there is a current content issue when {{highlight|none has been demonstrated}}.

In short: policy does not support tagging based on {{highlight|hypothetical, unsubstantiated, or historical speculation}} — particularly when no editor, including yourself, has identified any concrete violation after repeated invitations to do so.
}}|From multiple October 2025 comments at [[Wikipedia:Signs of AI writing/Examples/Talk:Arthur Katalayi|Talk:Arthur Katalayi]]}}

{{blockquote|{{fmbox|image=none|text=
“Reeks of LLM” is {{highlight|pure speculation}}. You may believe that statement strengthens your argument, but in practice it weakens it. It reinforces my concern that the discussion is becoming increasingly {{highlight|speculative rather than evidence-based}}.
----
You have not provided conclusive supporting evidence. You have speculated repeatedly, and you continue to {{highlight|rely on speculation}} rather than demonstrating identifiable policy violations or factual inaccuracies.

[...]

Quite frankly, {{highlight|unsupported speculation and subjective interpretation}} can be more problematic than AI itself, because at least AI concerns can be evaluated through evidence, sourcing, and verification standards.
----
The writing style of some editors may appear similar to the output of LLMs. The imposition of sanctions requires evidence beyond {{highlight|basic stylistic or linguistic indications}}. When evaluating possible LLM use, it is best to consider the full pattern of the editor's recent edits and whether the edits comply with core content policies.
----
I'm glad you helped. I understand what the guideline says. My point is that much of this discussion has moved beyond policy interpretation and into {{highlight|subjective speculation}} about tone, writing style, and {{highlight|assumptions regarding intent}}.

If there are factual inaccuracies, fabricated citations, or identifiable policy violations, point to them directly. Otherwise, repeatedly asserting that something “sounds like AI” {{highlight|is still speculation}} regardless of who drafted the guideline.

The issue is not whether LLM-generated article content is prohibited. The issue is whether conclusions are being drawn based on evidence or based on {{highlight|personal impressions}} and {{highlight|emotional interpretation of writing style}}.
----
Until consensus is reached through proper discussion and policy-based reasoning, I will continue removing warnings and templates that I believe are {{highlight|unsupported, speculative, or improperly applied}}. That includes AI-generation notices as well as criticisms such as “written like a resume,” which, if anything, further supports my argument that much of this discussion has shifted toward {{highlight|subjective interpretation of writing style}} rather than identifiable policy violations or factual inaccuracies.
}}|From multiple May 2026 comments at {{section link|Wikipedia:AI noticeboard#What is reasonable to do when text is identified by AI as LLM-generated, but editor insists it is not?}} }}

{{blockquote|{{fmbox|image=none|text=
Regarding the allegation that I used LLMs, I request that such claims be substantiated with evidence. {{highlight|Speculating about another editor's editing methods is not a valid content-based argument.}} [...]<br>

[...] enough with {{highlight|general and pointless accusations}}. I think you're the one who's incapable of being an editor if you can't express concrete things, but rather {{highlight|general concepts that have little meaning}}.
}}|From [[Wikipedia:Administrators' noticeboard/IncidentArchive1226#Constant denial of using AI|a June 2026 comment]] at [[Wikipedia:Administrators' noticeboard/Incidents]]}}

====<span class="anchor" id="Let's focus on"></span> Canned calls to focus on content instead of conduct====
{{shortcut|WP:LETSFOCUSON}}
{{for|non-AI-specific guidance about this|Wikipedia:Pearl-clutching}}
{{tmbox|image=none|text=Words to watch: {{strong|''accusing/accusations/accusatory'', ''dismissing/dismissals/dismissive'', ''instead of/rather than ...'', ''let's focus on ...''}} }}
In many cases, editors have responded to accusations of AI use by denying that they have used AI to write content ([[WP:AINOVIOLATION|or that Wikipedia prohibits doing so]]) and calling for discussions to instead be [[WP:LETSJUSTFIXIT|focused solely on the improvement of affected articles]]. Such editors often [[WP:ORCC|ask accusers to help them]] by [[WP:WHERESTHEAI|pointing out which contributions]] have [[WP:FIXEDAITONE|tone issues]] or other problems.

When told that the general ''pattern'' of their editing has been [[WP:LLMDISRUPT|disruptive]], they often [[WP:IDHT|dismiss the accusation]] as being merely [[WP:CAAAOS|speculative]], unfounded, or [[WP:AIWL|having no basis in policy]], and accuse their critics of acting in a manner that they believe is [[WP:Incivility|uncivil]], hostile, dismissive, unprofessional, counterproductive, or an affront to Wikipedia's collaborative nature. Persistence from others is interpreted as aggression; if challenged sufficiently they may state their intent to withdraw from the discussion as it has become "emotionally charged". Since the editor did not write this comment themselves, they will often continue the discussion or return to it shortly afterwards.

AI will seldom confirm that it's being used without the prior consent of the user, so if the response is vague, sidesteps the question or [[WP:AIACCUSERISMEAN|considers it to be a personal attack]], this may be a sign of AI use.

'''Examples'''
{{sidebox|text=
{{legend|yellow|Descriptions of accusers' behavior}}
{{legend|lightgreen|Suggested alternative actions}}
}}
{{blockquote|
